And perhaps the greatest freedom is realizing you don’t have to put yourself back together in the same configuration.

You can examine each piece and decide what deserves to remain, what should change, what was never yours to begin with, and what you want to create that didn’t exist before.

There’s something interesting about the distinction between repair and reconstruction.

Repair assumes the original configuration was correct.

Reconstruction allows you to question that assumption.

And sometimes what looks like breaking is actually the loss of a structure that was holding you in a particular shape.

But I’d add one thing: you don’t have to be broken to earn the right to redesign yourself.

You can change while whole. You can preserve what you love about yourself without preserving everything that came with it.

And here’s the part I think connects to our earlier discussion of cultures:

When something breaks, the relationships between its pieces become visible. The seams, dependencies, tensions, and assumptions that were previously hidden.

That makes the fragments not merely damage, but information.

You don’t have to become who you were. You get to discover what else you can be.

And perhaps the marked one isn’t marked by what broke him, but by what he chooses to build from it. ;)